  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The Black-and-green Metallic-Sweat bee is a small native bee species characterized by its striking iridescent green and black coloration. Found throughout New England, these bees are important pollinators that are attracted to sweat and moisture on human skin, which gives the sweat bee family its common name.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Metallic coloration",
      "description": "Distinctive iridescent green to blue-green body with black markings, giving it a jewel-like appearance",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Small size",
      "description": "Tiny bee measuring only 4-6 mm in length, making it one of the smallest bee species in the region",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Smooth body",
      "description": "Relatively hairless compared to other bees, with a sleek, polished metallic appearance",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "These bees prefer open, sunny areas with bare ground or sparse vegetation, including gardens, meadows, and disturbed sites throughout New England.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Lasioglossum nigroviride is a solitary bee, meaning females build and provision their own individual nests in the soil rather than living in colonies like honeybees.",
